WEBVTT

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.621
Hi guys, my name is Alini.
大家好，我叫阿利妮。

00:00:03.621 --> 00:00:07.600
I'm from Brazil and I've been a fan of the show for a few years now.
我来自巴西，这几年我一直是这个节目的粉丝。

00:00:07.599 --> 00:00:10.011
I first started watching when I was 16.
我最早是在16岁时开始看的。

00:00:10.011 --> 00:00:19.100
It was my senior year of high school and at the time I was having a lot of fear and anxiety because I had no idea what I wanted to do with my life.
那时我正读高中最后一年，当时我有很多恐惧和焦虑，因为我完全不知道自己的人生想要做什么。

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:31.100
I didn't know what profession I wanted or what college and the show really helped me to understand that not only my feelings were valid but also that I could trust myself.
我不知道自己想从事什么职业，也不知道该上什么大学，而这部剧真的帮我认识到，不仅我的感受是合理的，而且我也能相信自己。

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.460
and that I should trust the process that eventually things were going to work out.
并且我应该相信这个过程，最终一切都会好起来的。

00:00:35.179 --> 00:00:36.013
And now I'm 24.
而现在我24岁了。

00:00:36.013 --> 00:00:43.900
I'm a psychologist and I'm studying to get into residency, which has given me a lot of the same viewing reading thoughts that I had back then.
我是一名心理学家，正在备考住院医师，这让我产生了很多和当年一样的思考和感悟。

00:00:44.539 --> 00:00:50.162
And rewatching the show because of the podcast is really helping me through it again.
因为这个播客，重温这部剧真的再次帮我度过了那段时期。

00:00:50.162 --> 00:00:51.551
So thank you so much.
所以非常感谢你们。

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:58.100
Thanks for the great advice and thanks for always being that show that's there when I need comfort.
感谢你们提供的好建议，也感谢你们一直作为那个在我需要安慰时陪伴在侧的节目。

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:22.049
Welcome, welcome back to How We Made Your Mother.
欢迎，欢迎回到《老爸老妈的浪漫史》。

00:01:22.049 --> 00:01:23.049
I'm Josh Radner.
我是乔什·拉德纳。

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:25.129
I am joined by my friend Craig Thomas.
和我一起的是我的朋友克雷格·托马斯。

00:01:25.129 --> 00:01:26.129
Hello, Craig.
你好，克雷格。

00:01:26.129 --> 00:01:27.129
Hey, Josh.
嘿，乔什。

00:01:27.129 --> 00:01:28.129
Very happy to be with you.
很高兴能和你在一起。

00:01:28.129 --> 00:01:35.530
co-created a show called How I Met Your Mother with Carter Bayes that ran on CBS from 2005 to 2014.
我和卡特·贝斯共同创作了一部名为《老爸老妈的浪漫史》的剧集，于2005年至2014年在哥伦比亚广播公司播出。

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:37.090
I played Ted Mosby on that show.
我在剧中饰演泰德·莫斯比。

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:40.090
And we are doing a deep dive.
我们正在进行深度剖析。

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:41.569
We're revisiting the show.
我们在重温这部剧。

00:01:41.569 --> 00:01:45.689
All these many years later, we are discovering gems.
多年后的今天，我们正在发掘其中的珍宝。

00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:48.769
We're discovering things we would have done different.
我们正在发现那些我们本可以做得不同的事情。

00:01:48.769 --> 00:01:57.250
We're interrogating the mystery of why people from Brazil and all over the world continue to love this show.
我们在探讨为什么来自巴西和世界各地的人们会继续热爱这部剧的奥秘。

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:05.709
I will say that Ben Lee and I, as Radner and Lee, we toured Brazil in 2017, I think.
我要说的是，本·李和我，也就是“拉德纳与李”组合，大概在2017年去巴西巡演过。

00:02:05.709 --> 00:02:06.549
That's so cool.
太酷了。

00:02:06.549 --> 00:02:09.189
I don't think I ever, yeah, tell that story.
我想我以前好像，对，讲讲那个故事吧。

00:02:09.189 --> 00:02:14.629
It was the first time I really, I really learned that Brazilians love how I met your mother.
那是第一次我真正地、真正地了解到巴西人有多么喜爱《老爸老妈的浪漫史》。

00:02:14.629 --> 00:02:18.270
How I met your mother is huge, demonstrably huge in Brazil.
《老爸老妈的浪漫史》在巴西极其受欢迎，这是毋庸置疑的。

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:20.389
We've really heard over the years so many nice things.
这些年来，我们确实听到了太多美好的评价。

00:02:20.389 --> 00:02:23.229
And did you feel that love, Josh, when you were touring with Ben through there?
乔什，你和本在那里巡演时，有感受到那种热情吗？

00:02:23.229 --> 00:02:24.069
Is that what you're saying?
你是这个意思吗？

00:02:24.069 --> 00:02:24.909
Oh my God, yeah.
哦天哪，是的。

00:02:24.909 --> 00:02:26.469
It was pretty extraordinary.
简直太非同寻常了。

00:02:26.469 --> 00:02:36.590
I mean, our shows were incredibly packed and very, you know, some people weirdly like had, the album hadn't been out that long.
我的意思是，我们的演出座无虚席，而且你知道，奇怪的是有些人，那张专辑发行其实没多久。

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:38.069
People were really singing along.
人们真的跟着一起大合唱。

00:02:38.069 --> 00:02:44.949
But I also like, our shows were spoke to a bigger fan base than our band currently had.
但我也觉得，我们的演出吸引了比我们当时的乐队本身要庞大得多的粉丝群体。

00:02:44.949 --> 00:02:52.990
And I'm not saying that there weren't some deep Radner and Lee fans, but there were also how I met your mother fans there that were excited to hear the music.
我并不是说没有坚定的“拉德纳与李”的粉丝，但现场也有《老爸老妈的浪漫史》的粉丝，他们很高兴能听到这些音乐。

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:54.789
And it was really fun.
真的非常有趣。

00:02:54.789 --> 00:02:56.550
Yeah, it was a great time.
对，那是一段美好的时光。

00:02:56.550 --> 00:03:14.389
And, you know, it also, you know, the fact that, that this, the person who wrote the letter is studying to be a psychologist, I think also speaks to this thing of like, for those people who are, you can watch how I met your mother as like, this is just a good laugh, this is a good story.
而且，你知道，写这封信的人正在学习成为一名心理学家，我觉得这也说明了这样一点：对于那些把《老爸老妈的浪漫史》看作是一部能带来欢乐、讲了个好故事的人来说——

00:03:14.389 --> 00:03:22.990
But for people who are psychologically minded, I think that the show isn't particularly pleasing and also this podcast, I hope, is particularly pleasing.
但对于有心理学思维的人来说，我认为这部剧格外讨人喜欢，而且我也希望这个播客同样格外讨人喜欢。

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:28.297
We sometimes have guest appearances an actual psychologist on this show.
我们有时会在节目中邀请真正的心理学家作为嘉宾。

00:03:28.297 --> 00:03:48.642
I think that the reason, one of the reasons that the show tends to transcend geography and race and ethnicity is because it deals with these sweeping, psychologically universal concepts of how do we get older, how do we let go, how do we forgive, How do we form relationships?
我认为这部剧之所以能够超越地理、种族和国界的原因之一，是因为它探讨了这些宏大且具有心理学普遍性的概念：我们如何变老，如何放手，如何原谅，我们如何建立人际关系？

00:03:48.642 --> 00:03:50.706
How do we end relationships?
我们如何结束人际关系？

00:03:50.706 --> 00:03:56.234
I mean, these are things that it doesn't matter where you are, who you are.
我的意思是，无论你在哪里，无论你是谁，这些都是你会遇到的问题。

00:03:56.234 --> 00:04:00.509
These areas, you'll hit a lot of these areas in your life.
在你的生活中，你会在很多方面经历这些领域。

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:09.288
Yeah, I feel like the show, we hear a lot that it's people's comfort show, but almost paradoxically, I think it's also their take a leap show.
对，我觉得这部剧，我们经常听到它是人们的“慰藉之剧”，但几乎有些矛盾的是，我认为它也是他们的“勇敢一跃之剧”。

00:04:09.288 --> 00:04:13.615
She was just talking about two moments where the show helped her take a leap.
她刚才正是在谈论这部剧帮助她勇敢迈出两步的时刻。

00:04:13.615 --> 00:04:18.672
And there is that feeling of this is a comfort show, but it's also a show that progresses.
那种感觉是，这既是一部带来慰藉的剧，但同时也是一部不断前行的剧。

00:04:18.672 --> 00:04:19.627
It is about time.
是时候了。

00:04:19.627 --> 00:04:20.863
It is about evolution.
这是关于进化的。

00:04:20.863 --> 00:04:22.829
It is about becoming a perspective.
这是关于形成一种视角。

00:04:22.829 --> 00:04:30.104
The way we frame our life, the way we become aware that our life is a story that moves forward and we get to shape that narrative to some extent.
我们构建生活的方式，我们意识到生活是一个不断向前发展的的故事，并且我们在某种程度上可以塑造这个叙事。

00:04:30.104 --> 00:04:35.071
I love that part of her message just that she said it helped her in these moments of making a leap.
我非常喜欢她传达的信息中的那一部分，她说这在那些跨越转折的时刻帮助了她。

00:04:35.071 --> 00:04:39.837
And the fact that congratulations that you made this leap to this new career, that's wonderful.
事实上，祝贺你勇敢迈出这一步，开启了新的职业生涯，这太棒了。

00:04:39.837 --> 00:04:42.144
I'm so glad the show is there for you at time.
我很高兴这个节目能在当时陪伴着你。

00:04:42.144 --> 00:04:45.355
It's also interesting to interrogate the idea of a comfort show.
审视“慰藉剧”（comfort show）这个概念也很有意思。

00:04:45.355 --> 00:04:49.670
The fact that that how I met your mother, we hear it so much, is comforting to people.
事实上，就像《老爸老妈浪漫史》一样，我们经常听到它能给人们带来安慰。

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:52.509
Doesn't mean that the characters on the show are comfortable all the time.
这并不意味着剧中的角色一直都过得很舒适。

00:04:52.509 --> 00:04:55.870
In fact, the characters on the show are in deep discomfort a lot.
事实上，剧中的角色经常处于极度的痛苦之中。

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:56.790
They're mostly uncomfortable.
他们大部分时间都感到很不自在。

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:58.269
They're mostly uncomfortable.
他们大多数时候都过得很纠结。

00:04:58.269 --> 00:05:16.050
But what I think is comforting about it is that notion of perspective, is that notion of family and community being there alongside of you and knowing that in your discomfort, you, on the other side of that discomfort might be the life you're really longing for.
但我觉得它能带给人安慰的是那种视角观念，是那种家庭和社区就在你身边陪伴的感觉，并且让你知道，在你的痛苦之中，在痛苦的另一端，可能就是你真正渴望的生活。

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:17.769
Yeah, it's not a show of stasis.
对，这不是一部一成不变的剧。

00:05:17.769 --> 00:05:18.610
No.
没错。

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:20.649
The comfort of the show is not in stasis.
这部剧带给人的安慰并不在于一成不变。

00:05:20.649 --> 00:05:27.569
The comfort of the show, I think, is look at how fucked up all these people are, too, just like how I'm feeling fucked up.
我认为这部剧带给人的安慰在于：看啊，这群人也同样一团糟，就像我现在的感觉一样。

00:05:27.569 --> 00:05:34.209
And there's something about the flaws and the vulnerability of these characters that I think is comforting, even though it's a mess in a way, too.
这些角色的缺点和脆弱有一种魔力，我觉得这很抚慰人心，尽管某种程度上这也一团糟。

00:05:34.209 --> 00:05:46.649
It's almost like when you get to know someone you're really inspired by that you really think, like, has either a career that's enviable or a family life Or just a person in the world that you look at from afar and go, they've really figured something out.
这有点像当你去了解一个真正让你备受启发的人，你觉得他们拥有令人艳羡的职业生涯，或者美满的家庭生活，或者你只是远远地看着世上的某个人，心想：他们真的是把人生都盘算明白了。

00:05:46.649 --> 00:05:59.329
And then you get to know them over a couple meals, and you're like, oh my God, they're just as well as me, like they're just, they have the same boiling insecurities, the same childhood stuff, the same everything.
然后你和他们一起吃了几顿饭，深入了解之后，你会想：天哪，他们其实跟我差不多，他们也有同样沸腾的不安全感，同样的童年阴影，什么都一样。

00:05:59.329 --> 00:06:11.129
And you just realize like we're successful in spite of a lot of our child, not, you know, like, everyone, everyone has this stuff, you know, my friend Max always says this thing that I love.
你就会明白，我们取得成功其实恰恰克服了许多童年带来的影响，不，你知道的，每个人、每个人都有这些问题，你知道，我的朋友马克斯总是说的一句话我很喜欢。

00:06:11.129 --> 00:06:14.490
is actually told him that there's no such thing as a winner circle.
其实我告诉过他，根本没有什么赢家俱乐部。

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:23.649
You know, we think we're gonna get to a place where we're just gonna feel resolved and successful and I finally figured it out and cracked the champagne and it just never happens.
你知道的，我们以为自己会到达一个地方，在那里我们会感到一切都尘埃落定、获得成功，终于把人生琢磨透了，然后开香槟庆祝，但这从未发生过。

00:06:23.649 --> 00:06:25.810
But it's the great fallacy.
但这却是一个巨大的谬论。

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:28.529
It's the great fallacy chasing that saying that does not exist.
追逐那个并不存在的说法，就是最大的谬论。

00:06:28.529 --> 00:06:36.610
And I think that in some ways it's the overarching narrative of the show is the main character was obsessed with the finish line.
我认为在某些方面，这也是这部剧的整体叙事——主角痴迷于终点线。

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:38.129
He's obsessed with the goal post.
他痴迷于目标。

00:06:38.129 --> 00:06:47.009
He even, even Marshall Lilly, the front porch, There's this idea that there's a moment when everyone's gonna exhale and just high five.
甚至，甚至是马歇尔和莉莉，还有那个前廊，大家总觉得会有那么一个时刻，所有人都会如释重负地松一口气，然后击掌庆贺。

00:06:47.009 --> 00:06:56.129
But the truth of it was the thing that was the best was the, it was true, I mean it's the cliche of cliches, but it was the journey, it was the thing all along.
但事实是，最美好的东西其实是……没错，这是老生常谈的陈词滥调，但旅程本身、过程本身就是一切。

00:06:56.129 --> 00:07:00.970
That's why you could have sold the story in 15 minutes is like, stop saying that, like no.
这就是为什么你本可以在15分钟内就把这个故事讲完，就像在说：别再唠叨了，根本不是这样的。

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:09.930
The whole thing is it had to be unspooled in this long ornate kind of narrative because that's where the gold is.
重点在于，它必须以这种漫长而华丽的叙事方式一点点展开，因为那才是真正的金矿所在。

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:10.769
Yeah, it's funny.
对，这挺有意思的。

00:07:10.769 --> 00:07:15.170
Sometimes we get that comment from people on the internet, like, I love the show, but it could have all been told in 15 minutes.
有时我们在网上看到网友这样的评论：我喜欢这部剧，但其实15分钟就能讲完。

00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:17.930
And I'm like, then there would have been no show for you to love.
而我想说的是，那这样的话，就不会有一部让你深爱的剧了。

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:19.930
So I guess it was okay to stretch it out.
所以我想，把它拉长也是可以的吧。

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:27.649
Well, all of those flaws and all of those emotional ups and downs are well-on exhibit.
嗯，所有这些缺点以及所有的情感起伏都展露无遗。

00:07:27.649 --> 00:07:30.529
In today's episode, we're discussing, that was a transition.
在今天的节目中，我们要讨论的是……这真是一个神转折。

00:07:30.529 --> 00:07:31.449
It was very good.
非常棒。

00:07:31.449 --> 00:07:32.290
It was very good.
非常棒。

00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:33.129
You guys feel that?
你们感觉到了吗？

00:07:33.129 --> 00:07:35.329
That was professional podcasting right there.
这绝对是专业级别的播客。

00:07:35.329 --> 00:07:38.370
Season two, man, for figuring it out.
第二季了嘛，总算摸清门道了。

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:42.490
Alec, tell us what episode we're talking about, when it aired and who wrote it, yeah?
亚历克，给我们讲讲我们今天要讨论的是哪一集，它是何时播出的，又是谁写的，好吗？

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:43.329
Absolutely.
当然。

00:07:43.329 --> 00:07:45.810
This is the Scorpion and the Toad.
这一集是《蝎子与蟾蜍》（The Scorpion and the Toad）。

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:53.970
It originally aired on September 25th, 25, 2006, and it was written by Chris Harris.
它最初于2006年9月25日播出，由克里斯·哈里斯（Chris Harris）编剧。

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:55.170
Chris Harris.
克里斯·哈里斯。

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:57.089
Chris Harris, one of the all-timers.
克里斯·哈里斯，绝对是史上最强编剧之一。

00:07:57.089 --> 00:08:04.930
If you're just tuning in for season two, we had Chris Harris' guest on the show, some going deep into season one, or maybe midway through it.
如果你是从第二季才开始收听的，我们在节目中曾邀请过克里斯·哈里斯作为嘉宾，深入探讨过第一季的内容，或者大概在第一季播到中段的时候。

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:21.509
Yeah, for the episode Game Night, if you haven't heard that, Well, go back and listen to Olive season one if you haven't heard it of this podcast, having made your mother a really, Chris was on and talked, we talked in depth about the episode, Game Nighting, but also just about, you know, I embarrassed Chris, Chris always turns red if you watch, if you can watch the YouTube clip, if he just turns red.
是的，对于“游戏之夜”那一集，如果你还没听过，去找来听听看，第一季的《老爸老妈浪漫史》如果你还没听过这个播客的话，克里斯曾上过节目，我们深入聊了“游戏之夜”那一集，还有，你知道的，我让克里斯很尴尬，如果你看YouTube片段的话，克里斯总是会脸红，他只要一上节目就会脸红。

00:08:21.509 --> 00:08:22.709
He still has them when he turns red.
他脸红的时候依然很帅。

00:08:22.709 --> 00:08:48.710
He's still very handsome, but he, yeah, well, he really is one of the all-time best premium writers and he, he just had this knack and you see it in this episode of just being very playful and clever and really as much as anyone on the staff, including Carter and myself, really using the narrator to shape jokes, really using the storytelling kind of superpowers the toolkit of how you can tell stories and how much mother Chris is really good at that.
他依然非常帅气，而且，是的，他真的是史上最顶级的优秀编剧之一，他有一种天赋，你在这集里也能看到，那就是非常调皮、聪明，而且和剧组里的任何人一样，包括卡特和我，真的会利用旁白来构思笑话，真的在运用讲故事的超能力，也就是如何讲故事的工具箱，克里斯在这方面真的很擅长。

00:08:48.710 --> 00:09:00.549
One example in this episode, I really liked the cleverness of seeing the first version of Lilly's story of how great San Francisco was for her on the bus with all these wonderful characters.
这集里有一个例子，我真的很喜欢那种巧妙的设计，就是看到莉莉关于三藩市对她有多棒的第一个版本的故事，车上坐着各种各样美好的人物。

00:09:00.549 --> 00:09:09.149
And then the reveal later that all the same dialogue just kind of out of one person's mouth, who is just a really weird kind of creepy dude that she was sitting next to on the bus.
然后后来揭晓，所有相同的对话其实都是从一个人嘴里说出来的，那个人只是她在车上旁边坐着的一个非常古怪、有点让人起鸡皮疙瘩的家伙。

00:09:09.149 --> 00:09:11.789
That's, that's a, that feels like a very Chris Harris joke.
这，这感觉非常像是克里斯·哈里斯式的笑话。

00:09:11.789 --> 00:09:16.990
Just the idea of like all of those words will perfectly mean something entirely different the second time.
就是那种所有这些词在第二次出现时会完全变成另一种意思的创意。

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:21.269
Well, also with the art teacher, that what he says, it's literally the same dialogue with.
还有美术老师那段，他说的话，字面上的台词完全是一样的。

00:09:21.269 --> 00:09:23.190
Yeah, yeah, yeah, sorry, I mentioned that whole montage.
对，对，对，抱歉，我刚才说的是整个蒙太奇。

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:25.990
Yeah, it's literally, it's all the words are the same for all of those beats.
对， literalmente（字面意思），所有这些桥段里的词都是一样的。

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:26.830
Yeah, I love that.
是的，我太喜欢那个了。

00:09:26.830 --> 00:09:32.879
It reminds me of, have you seen that thing online that's like, will you marry me as a marriage proposal?
这让我想起，你在网上见过那种梗吗，比如“will you marry me”（你愿意嫁给我吗）作为一个求婚？

00:09:33.559 --> 00:09:43.659
And then it's, will you marry me is a proposal for a for some like will you marry and me.
然后它是，“will you marry me”（Will，你娶我吧）变成了对某个像will（威尔）和mary（玛丽）的人的提议。

00:09:43.659 --> 00:09:44.659
Oh my God.
天哪。

00:09:44.659 --> 00:09:45.659
That's a good one.
那个真不错。

00:09:45.659 --> 00:09:54.659
And then it was like, will you marry me is like a caveman introduction or something like will you marry me?
然后还有，“will you marry me”就像是原始人的自我介绍什么的，比如“will, you marry me”（威尔，你嫁给我吧）。

00:09:54.659 --> 00:09:56.659
Mary's introducing yourself to will.
玛丽在向威尔做自我介绍。

00:09:56.659 --> 00:09:57.659
The caveman.
那个原始人。

00:09:57.659 --> 00:09:58.659
Like will you marry me?
就像“will you marry me”？

00:09:58.659 --> 00:10:02.659
Like basically I don't wear the stresses where the comma is where that but he does that.
基本上就是重音落在哪里，逗号在哪里，但他就是能做到这一点。

00:10:02.659 --> 00:10:10.059
especially with the art teacher, I actually heard it, and I was like, it's so smart, the way the art teacher is flipped, you know?
尤其是美术老师那段，我当时听到的时候就想，美术老师那段的反转真的太聪明了，你知道吗？

00:10:10.059 --> 00:10:13.088
Can I say that we got a comment from Jen J.
我能说我们收到了一条来自珍·J·坦（Jen J. Tan）的评论吗？

00:10:13.088 --> 00:10:23.299
Tan who said, I'm an English teacher and have used the two contrasting clips of Lillian San Francisco to help my students practice pronunciation.
她说：“我是一名英语老师，我用莉莉在三藩市那两个对比鲜明的片段来帮助我的学生练习发音。”

00:10:23.299 --> 00:10:30.899
It's a great example of how changing word stress and emphasis can really affect the meaning of what you say, even when the words may be exactly the same.
这是一个很好的例子，说明改变单词的重音和语调如何真正影响你所说的话的含义，即使这些词可能完全一样。

00:10:30.899 --> 00:10:32.820
Yeah, Chris is really good at that stuff.
是的，克里斯在处理这些事情上真的很有一套。

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:40.500
He's written some really brilliant children's books, but they're like Shel Silverstein, Oscar for Adults 2, and he's just very playful with words.
他写过一些非常棒的儿童读物，有点像谢尔·西尔弗斯坦（Shel Silverstein）或成人版的奥斯卡2，而且他对文字的玩味非常浓厚。

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:43.539
And I think he really, you really see that in this episode.
我觉得在这一集中，你真的能深深地感受到这一点。

00:10:43.539 --> 00:10:47.740
I mean, I'm also delighted that how I met your mother is being used to educate the youth of America.
我的意思是，我也很高兴看到《老爸老妈浪漫史》被用来教育美国的青少年。

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:50.100
That's amazing, I know, I skipped right over that.
太惊人了，我知道，我刚才直接忽略了这一点。

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:59.620
We hear it all the time that people learn English from the show, people study it in classes and college and stuff, it's very cool for our little CBS sitcom from 2005.
我们经常听到有人通过这部剧学习英语，有人在课程和大学里研究它，对我们这个2005年出品的小小CBS情景喜剧来说，这真的很酷。

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:05.019
This is jumping a little bit ahead, but I wonder what a non-native English speaker would say.
这稍微有点提前，但我很好奇非母语英语学习者会怎么说。

00:11:05.019 --> 00:11:06.340
What is redonk?
什么是redonk？

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:19.340
And I wrote you this little day to send you in New York City.
我给你写了这段小日程，让你在纽约市发送。

00:11:19.340 --> 00:11:30.039
We'll be right back.
我们马上回来。

00:11:30.039 --> 00:11:32.240
And now, back to the show.
现在，言归正传。

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:39.590
Craig, just give us a little armchair summary of what this episode's about, where we left our heroes and where we are today.
克雷格，请给我们大致总结一下这一集的内容，以及我们的主角们之前在什么地方，今天又处于什么状态。

00:11:39.590 --> 00:11:48.429
Yeah, so we, episode one of season two ends with this reveal that Lily is back from San Francisco, but nobody knows yet.
好的，第二季第一集以莉莉从旧金山回来但还没人知道的悬念结束。

00:11:48.429 --> 00:11:51.110
Marshall has been a terrible mess all summer.
整个夏天马修都一团糟。

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:53.470
He's finally starting to show some signs of life.
他终于开始显露出一些生机了。

00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:57.269
And so we meet him in this episode where he says, I got to get back out there somehow.
所以在这一集里我们看到他说：‘我无论如何得重新振作起来。’

00:11:57.269 --> 00:12:02.789
Barney is very excited to be the wingman to Marshall now that Ted's dead to him because Ted's in relationship with Robin.
因为泰德跟罗宾在一起了，在巴尼眼里泰德已经‘死’了，所以巴尼非常兴奋能成为马修的僚机。

00:12:02.789 --> 00:12:04.750
So Barney's like, it's you now, Marshall.
因此巴尼觉得：‘现在是你了，马修。’

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:05.750
It's all been leading here.
一切都是为了这一刻。

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:06.857
you're my guy.
‘你就是我的搭档。’

00:12:06.857 --> 00:12:12.629
So Barney takes Marshall under his wing to get out there and meet a lady.
于是巴尼把马修纳入羽翼之下，带他出去结识女士。

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:14.394
It's based.
这就对了。

00:12:14.394 --> 00:12:19.374
The reason it's called the scorpion on the toad is there's a parable of the scorpion on the toad.
这一集之所以叫《蝎子与蟾蜍》，是因为有一个关于蝎子和蟾蜍的寓言。

00:12:19.374 --> 00:12:23.429
That is, I guess I'll just say it because we never explained it in the episode.
我想我就直说了吧，因为我们在剧里从来没解释过。

00:12:23.429 --> 00:12:26.749
It is there's a scorpion and there's a toad and there's a stream.
大意就是有一只蝎子、一只蟾蜍和一条小溪。

00:12:26.749 --> 00:12:28.230
There's a river or something.
一条河什么的。

00:12:28.230 --> 00:12:32.137
And the scorpion says, I got to get to the other side.
蝎子说，我得去河对岸。

00:12:32.137 --> 00:12:34.164
Can I ride on you, Mr. Toad?
我能搭你的背吗，蟾蜍先生？

00:12:34.164 --> 00:12:39.229
And Mr. Toad says, Yes, but if you were to sting me, we will both die.
蟾蜍先生说，可以，但如果你蜇我，我们都会死。

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:42.629
And the scorpion says, I'm not gonna sting you.
蝎子说，我不会蜇你的。

00:12:42.629 --> 00:12:44.429
Come on, we'll just be a team.
来吧，我们组个团队。

00:12:44.429 --> 00:12:46.429
We'll just get across the stream together.
我们一起过河就行了。

00:12:46.429 --> 00:12:50.470
And the toad, the sweet, sweet portote goes, okay.
然后那只蟾蜍，那只心地善良的好蟾蜍说，好吧。

00:12:50.470 --> 00:12:54.470
And he puts the scorpion in his back and starts swimming across and halfway across the river.
它把蝎子背在背上开始游过去，游到河中央时。

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:55.990
The scorpion stings him.
蝎子蜇了它。

00:12:55.990 --> 00:13:00.549
And as they both start to drown, the toad says, why did you do that?
当两人开始下沉时，蟾蜍问，你为什么要这么做？

00:13:00.549 --> 00:13:03.629
And the scorpion goes, because it's my nature.
蝎子说，因为这就是我的天性。

00:13:03.629 --> 00:13:04.830
and they both die.
然后两人都死了。

00:13:06.509 --> 00:13:09.429
Look, and Barney is the scorpion in the sense.
你看，从某种意义上说，巴尼就是那只蝎子。

00:13:09.429 --> 00:13:23.110
Yeah, you know, it's, it just, it continues to delight me that you can just watch how I met your mother as a show about a bunch of people in New York City at a certain time looking for love and you know, growing up.
是的，你知道，我仍然感到非常高兴的是，你可以把《老爸老妈浪漫史》当成一部讲述纽约市一群人在特定时期寻找爱情和成长的剧集来看。

00:13:24.350 --> 00:13:27.870
And you can also say, hey, that's John Steinbeck reference.
你也可以说，嘿，那是约翰·斯坦贝克的梗。

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:29.629
That's F Scott's Fitzgerald.
那是F·斯科特·菲茨杰拉德。

00:13:29.629 --> 00:13:31.149
That's an ASAP fable.
那是伊索寓言。

00:13:31.149 --> 00:13:37.950
Like, there's, you give people multiple layers through which to experience and enjoy this thing.
就像，你给人们提供了多层体验和享受这部剧的方式。

00:13:37.950 --> 00:13:51.179
And for people who are alert to those Easter eggs, you know, there's the, I know what, I think it's the episode of Marshall, and I won't spoil anything, but there's an episode where it's just a countdown, like visual countdown.
对于那些留意这些彩蛋的人来说，你知道，有一个，我记得是马歇尔的那集，我不会剧透任何内容，但有一集里面有一个倒计时，类似于视觉上的倒计时。

00:13:51.179 --> 00:13:54.340
You see all these numbers ticking, ticking, ticking down.
你能看到所有这些数字在滴答滴答地倒数。

00:13:54.340 --> 00:13:57.179
And a lot of people didn't notice that on the first watch.
很多人在第一次看的时候都没有注意到这一点。

00:13:57.179 --> 00:14:02.059
That's something that you can get alert to, because it's not overly, you know, you don't hit him over the head.
这是你可以去留心发现的东西，因为它不是很过分，你知道，它不会直接拍到你脸上去。

00:14:02.059 --> 00:14:15.940
And I think that respecting the intelligence of the viewer and not leading them in a way that was insulting, but you know, like Billy Wilder said, like letting them put two and two together, I think that's part of the pleasure of the show, especially for repeat viewings.
而且我认为尊重观众的智力，不以一种侮辱性的方式引导他们，而是像比利·怀尔德说的那样，让他们自己把线索拼凑起来，我认为这正是这部剧的乐趣所在，尤其是对于重复观看的人来说。

00:14:15.940 --> 00:14:17.740
Yeah, yeah, I hope so.
对，对，我希望如此。

00:14:17.740 --> 00:14:18.799
That was definitely the goal.
这绝对是我们的目标。

00:14:18.799 --> 00:14:20.019
It's nice to hear you say that.
很高兴听到你这么说。

00:14:20.019 --> 00:14:22.779
I think we did all make that show.
我觉得我们确实都参与制作了那样一部剧。

00:14:24.139 --> 00:14:46.220
And yeah, I was impressed, and other than instruct me about that parable and how that episode kind of plays with that parable, is it's funny, it's season two, we're trying to still build an audience, we're still getting a lot of notes, like make sure everyone's likable, make sure, make sure the show fits right in there with King of Queens and two and a half men, and it's just like, ah, it's just a fun show.
是的，我印象深刻，除了告诉我那个寓言以及那一集是如何运用那个寓言的之外，很有趣的是，那是第二季，我们还在努力建立观众群，我们仍然收到很多意见，比如确保每个人都讨人喜欢，确保这部剧能完美融入《后中之王》和《好汉两个半》这样的风格中，感觉就像，啊，这只是一部有趣的剧。

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:54.659
But essentially this episode, and this is the daring of this episode, and it really struck me, essentially this episode's question is like, is Barney a sociopath?
但本质上这一集，这也是这一集的冒险之处，它真的打动了我，本质上这一集的问题就像是，巴尼是个反社会者吗？

00:14:54.659 --> 00:15:08.419
Like this episode is essentially like American psycho, you know, I mean, like Barney cannot stop himself from continually stealing these women that he knows Marshall's a mess.
就像这一集本质上就像《美国精神病人》，你知道的，我是说，巴尼无法克制自己，不断地去抢这些女人，他明明知道马歇尔现在一团糟。

00:15:08.419 --> 00:15:09.980
He knows Marshall needs a woman.
他知道马歇尔需要一个女人。

00:15:09.980 --> 00:15:11.460
He knows he wants that for Marshall.
他知道他希望马歇尔得到幸福。

00:15:11.460 --> 00:15:19.220
He loves Marshall, but there's something in his nature that he just cannot resist the chance to have a conquest of his own.
他爱马歇尔，但他的天性中有些东西让他无法抗拒去征服的诱惑。

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:30.409
And of course at the end, there's that kind of like moral debate, philosophical debate, which is probably bullshit, but Barney kind of implies, but maybe it's not entirely bullshit.
当然在最后，有一种类似道德辩论、哲学辩论的东西，这可能都是废话，但巴尼暗示过，不过也许这并非完全是废话。

00:15:30.409 --> 00:15:33.169
Kind of implies, I'm doing this for your own good.
有点暗示说，我这么做是为了你好。

00:15:33.169 --> 00:15:40.929
I told you being single was so funny, it's like being at a candy store, it's not, it's a post-apocalyptic wasteland, it's every man for himself, and that's the best lesson you.
我告诉过你单身有多有趣，就像在糖果店一样，其实不然，这是个末日后的废土，每个人都在为自己而战，这是给你最好的教训。

00:15:40.929 --> 00:15:44.889
That's what you need to learn, having lived in a captivity of couplehood for so long.
这就是你需要学到的，在长期处于恋爱关系的囚禁中生活之后。

00:15:44.889 --> 00:15:51.309
And I think Barney on some level does think he's offering up that lesson, but also he just wants to steal these women.
我认为巴尼在某种程度上确实认为他是在提供这一课，但同时他只是想抢走这些女人。

00:15:51.309 --> 00:15:54.409
And it's just so compulsive and like really crazy of him.
这太强迫性了，而且他真的很疯狂。

00:15:54.409 --> 00:15:55.690
But it's just so funny.
但这也太搞笑了。

00:15:55.690 --> 00:15:58.085
It's kind of a dark, it's a dark storyline.
这有点黑暗，这是一个黑暗的故事线。

00:15:58.085 --> 00:15:59.867
It's really weird the storyline.
这条故事线真的很奇怪。

00:15:59.867 --> 00:16:08.667
On this topic, our friend Swarly memes says, without giving away Barney's real motive later this season, I wanted to ask about the psychology in this episode.
关于这个话题，我们的朋友斯瓦利迷（Swarly memes）说，在不透露本季后面巴尼的真实动机的情况下，我想问一下这一集里的心理学。

00:16:08.667 --> 00:16:12.510
Barney keeps blocking Marshall even though it hurts their friendship.
巴尼不断阻挠马歇尔，尽管这伤害了他们的友谊。

00:16:12.510 --> 00:16:15.963
The fable says the scorpion stings because it's in his nature.
寓言里说蝎子蜇人是因为这是它的天性。

00:16:15.963 --> 00:16:30.109
Did you write Barney that way as someone who can't escape his nature or someone making a choice to hurt Marshall anyway, or neither him spoilers, and weren't you a bit scared to make Barney come across almost like a psychopath by making him the scorpion?
你把巴尼写成这样一个无法摆脱天性的人，还是一个无论如何都要选择伤害马歇尔的人，或者都不是？还有剧透一下，把巴尼写得像个蝎子，让他看起来几乎像个精神病患者，你当时难道不有点害怕吗？

00:16:30.109 --> 00:16:31.390
Please talk about this.
请谈谈这个。

00:16:31.390 --> 00:16:45.202
I'm surprised that we did it this early in season two because we probably, I bet the network would have preferred we did an episode where Barney was less evil than this episode, David, just to be like, let's get more people in and liking everybody.
我很惊讶我们在第二季这么早就做了这一集，因为我敢说，电视网可能更希望我们拍一集巴尼不那么邪恶的剧集，大卫，只是为了吸引更多人来看并且喜欢每一个人。

00:16:45.202 --> 00:16:51.049
But I think the episode, it has this kind of tension and it's funny because we went to start with Barney.
但我认为这一集，它有这种张力，而且很有趣，因为我们是从巴尼开始的。

00:16:51.049 --> 00:16:54.094
And I think he can't stop himself at this moment.
我觉得此刻他无法控制自己。

00:16:54.094 --> 00:17:00.306
Through the long arc of the entire series, you see Barney try to be better than this at other times.
贯穿整部剧集的漫长发展，你会看到巴尼在其他时候试图表现得更好。

00:17:00.306 --> 00:17:02.169
But right here, he's just not.
但就在此时此刻，他并没有这样做。

00:17:02.169 --> 00:17:08.250
In some ways, it is a kinder narrative to speak of it in terms of compulsion or addiction.
从某种程度上讲，如果从强迫症或上瘾的角度来谈论它，这是一种更温和的叙事方式。

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:14.089
And because if he was actually making a choice, it's almost worse.
因为如果他是真的在做选择，那几乎就更糟了。

00:17:14.089 --> 00:17:17.809
It's like you're aware of what you're doing and you're hurt.
就像你知道自己在做什么，而且你受到了伤害。

00:17:17.809 --> 00:17:20.210
But there's something about the compulsivity of it.
但这种强迫行为本身有些特别之处。

00:17:20.210 --> 00:17:24.589
It's like, to put it in like addiction terms, he's powerless.
这就好比，用成瘾的话来说，他是无能为力的。

00:17:24.589 --> 00:17:28.450
He's powerless, you know, and- And the scorpion dies.
他无能为力，你知道，而且——蝎子死了。

00:17:28.450 --> 00:17:32.289
The scorpion is powerless to stop himself and dies because he can't stop himself.
蝎子无法阻止自己，最终死去，因为他控制不住自己。

00:17:32.289 --> 00:17:34.410
So it is that part of it too.
所以这其中也有这部分原因。

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:42.518
And Barney at the end, you know, crashes and burns gets his comeuppance and has spent, you know, there's, we don't see him do this exact thing in Marshall.
而且巴尼在最后，你知道的，自食恶果、遭到报应，并且花时间——你知道的，我们没看到他在马歇尔身上做过这种具体的事。

00:17:42.518 --> 00:17:44.872
This is the one episode where he's like this.
这是他表现成这样的唯一一集。

00:17:44.872 --> 00:17:53.504
But yeah, I would say that that's a good question because I think we were a little nervous doing this, but we were enamored of this idea of playing out this parable.
